<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-basis-dimension" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Basis and Dimension</title>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-basis">
    <title>Basis of a Subspace</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-basis">
      <title>Basis</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>H</m> be a subspace of <m>\R^n</m>. Then
          <m>\{ \vec{u}_1, \ldots, \vec{u}_k \}</m> is called a <term>basis</term> for
          <m>H</m> if the following conditions hold:
        </p>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li><p><m>\operatorname{Span}\{ \vec{u}_1, \ldots, \vec{u}_k \} = H</m>;</p></li>
            <li><p><m>\{ \vec{u}_1, \ldots, \vec{u}_k \}</m> is linearly independent.</p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <example xml:id="eg-two-bases">
      <title>Two Different Bases for the Same Subspace</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider the <m>xy</m>-plane, i.e.,
          <m>H = \left\{ \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{bmatrix} \in \R^3 \; \middle| \; z = 0 \right\}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The most obvious basis is
          <m>\mathcal{B}_1 = \{ \vec{e}_1, \vec{e}_2 \}</m>, since
          <m>H = \operatorname{Span}\{ \vec{e}_1, \vec{e}_2 \}</m> and
          <md>
            \vec{x} = x\vec{e}_1 + y\vec{e}_2.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          But it is not the only one. Take
          <m>\mathcal{B}_2 = \{ \vec{e}_1, \vec{e}_1 + \vec{e}_2 \}</m>. Then
          <m>H = \operatorname{Span}\{ \vec{e}_1, \vec{e}_1 + \vec{e}_2 \}</m> and
          <md>
            \vec{x} = (x - y)\vec{e}_1 + y \left( \vec{e}_1 + \vec{e}_2 \right).
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          A subspace has many bases <mdash /> but, as we are about to see, they all have
          the same size.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <figure xml:id="fig-two-bases">
      <caption>
        Two different bases for the <m>xy</m>-plane in <m>\R^3</m>:
        <m>\mathcal{B}_1 = \{ \vec{e}_1, \vec{e}_2 \}</m> on the left and
        <m>\mathcal{B}_2 = \{ \vec{e}_1, \vec{e}_1 + \vec{e}_2 \}</m> on the right.
      </caption>
      <image width="88%">
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.95, every node/.style={font=\small}]
            \foreach \sh in {0, 7.2} {
              \begin{scope}[xshift=\sh cm]
                \fill[black!10] (-1.7,-0.8) -- (3.3,-0.8) -- (4.4,1.2) -- (-0.6,1.2) -- cycle;
                \draw[black!35] (-1.7,-0.8) -- (3.3,-0.8) -- (4.4,1.2) -- (-0.6,1.2) -- cycle;
                \coordinate (O) at (1.35,0.2);
                \draw[-{Stealth[length=2mm]}] (O) -- ++(0,1.6) node[above] {$z$};
                \draw[-{Stealth[length=2mm]}] (O) -- ++(1.5,0) node[right] {$y$};
                \draw[-{Stealth[length=2mm]}] (O) -- ++(-0.85,-0.75) node[left] {$x$};
                \node at (3.7,-0.55) {$H$};
              \end{scope}
            }
            \begin{scope}
              \coordinate (O) at (1.35,0.2);
              \draw[magenta, very thick, -{Stealth[length=2mm]}] (O) -- ++(-0.55,-0.48)
                node[below left, black] {$\vec{e}_1$};
              \draw[magenta, very thick, -{Stealth[length=2mm]}] (O) -- ++(0.95,0)
                node[above, black] {$\vec{e}_2$};
            \end{scope}
            \begin{scope}[xshift=7.2cm]
              \coordinate (O) at (1.35,0.2);
              \draw[blue, very thick, -{Stealth[length=2mm]}] (O) -- ++(-0.55,-0.48)
                node[below left, black] {$\vec{e}_1$};
              \draw[blue, very thick, -{Stealth[length=2mm]}] (O) -- ++(0.40,-0.48)
                node[below right, black] {$\vec{e}_1+\vec{e}_2$};
            \end{scope}
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-all-bases-same-size">
    <title>All Bases Have the Same Size</title>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-spanning-vs-independent">
      <title>Spanning Sets Are at Least as Large as Independent Sets</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose <m>\{ \vec{u}_1, \ldots, \vec{u}_r \}</m> is a linearly independent set
          of vectors in <m>\R^n</m>, and each <m>\vec{u}_k</m> is contained in
          <m>\operatorname{Span}\{ \vec{v}_1, \ldots, \vec{v}_s \}</m>. Then
          <m>s \geq r</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          In words, spanning sets have at least as many vectors as linearly independent
          sets.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Since each <m>\vec{u}_j</m> is in
          <m>\operatorname{Span}\{ \vec{v}_1, \ldots, \vec{v}_s \}</m>, there exist
          scalars <m>a_{ij}</m> such that
          <md>
            \vec{u}_j = \sum_{i=1}^{s} a_{ij} \vec{v}_i.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Suppose for a contradiction that <m>s \lt r</m>. Then the matrix
          <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} a_{ij} \end{bmatrix}</m> has fewer rows, <m>s</m>, than
          columns, <m>r</m>. Then the system <m>AX = 0</m> has a nontrivial solution
          <m>\vec{d}</m>; that is, there is a <m>\vec{d} \neq \vec{0}</m> such that
          <m>A\vec{d} = \vec{0}</m>. In other words,
          <md>
            \sum_{j=1}^{r} a_{ij} d_j = 0, \qquad i = 1, 2, \ldots, s.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Therefore
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \sum_{j=1}^{r} d_j \vec{u}_j
              \amp = \sum_{j=1}^{r} d_j \sum_{i=1}^{s} a_{ij} \vec{v}_i
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = \sum_{i=1}^{s} \left( \sum_{j=1}^{r} a_{ij} d_j \right) \vec{v}_i
              = \sum_{i=1}^{s} 0 \vec{v}_i = \vec{0},
            </mrow>
          </md>
          which contradicts the assumption that
          <m>\{ \vec{u}_1, \ldots, \vec{u}_r \}</m> is linearly independent, because not
          all the <m>d_j</m> are zero. Thus this contradiction indicates that
          <m>s \geq r</m>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-bases-same-size">
      <title>Any Two Bases Have the Same Size</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>H</m> be a subspace of <m>\R^n</m> and suppose
          <m>\{ \vec{u}_1, \ldots, \vec{u}_r \}</m> and
          <m>\{ \vec{v}_1, \ldots, \vec{v}_s \}</m> are two bases for <m>H</m>. Then
          <m>r = s</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Note that any spanning set has at least as many vectors as a basis, by
          <xref ref="thm-spanning-vs-independent" text="type-global" />. Also, any basis
          is itself a spanning set. Hence:
        </p>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>\{ \vec{u}_1, \ldots, \vec{u}_r \}</m> is a basis and
                <m>\{ \vec{v}_1, \ldots, \vec{v}_s \}</m> is a spanning set, therefore
                <m>r \leq s</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>\{ \vec{v}_1, \ldots, \vec{v}_s \}</m> is a basis and
                <m>\{ \vec{u}_1, \ldots, \vec{u}_r \}</m> is a spanning set, therefore
                <m>s \leq r</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
        <p>
          Together these give <m>r = s</m>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-dimension">
    <title>Dimension</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-dimension">
      <title>Dimension</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>H</m> be a subspace of <m>\R^n</m>. Then the <term>dimension</term> of
          <m>H</m> is the number of vectors in a basis of <m>H</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <remark xml:id="rem-dimension-well-defined">
      <p>
        This definition makes sense only because of
        <xref ref="thm-bases-same-size" text="type-global" />: different bases could
        otherwise give different answers.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <example xml:id="eg-dimension">
      <title>Computing Dimensions</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the dimension of the following subspaces of <m>\R^3</m>?
        </p>
        <p>
          <md>
            H_1 = \left\{ \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{bmatrix} \in \R^3 \; \middle| \; x = y = z \right\},
            \qquad
            H_2 = \left\{ \vec{u} \in \R^3 \; \middle| \; \vec{u} \cdot \vec{v} = 0, \;
              \vec{v} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ -1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} \right\}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>H_1</m> is a line through the origin with the direction vector
          <m>\vec{v} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m> and
          <m>H_1 = \operatorname{Span}\{ \vec{v} \}</m>, hence
          <md>
            \dim H_1 = 1.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          For <m>H_2</m>, write <m>\vec{u} = \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{bmatrix}</m>.
          Then <m>\vec{u} \cdot \vec{v} = 2x - y + z = 0</m>, which is a plane through the
          origin, and
          <md>
            H_2 = \operatorname{Span}\left\{
              \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix},
              \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix} \right\}.
          </md>
          Note that any two vectors on the plane that are linearly independent can be used
          here. Hence
          <md>
            \dim H_2 = 2.
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>
</section>
